  p("U1","1.2","Displacement, Velocity, and Acceleration","2.A","An object starts with velocity v0 and has constant acceleration a for time t. Which expression gives its final velocity?","v = v0 + at",["v = v0t + a","v = v0 + a/t","v = v0 + at^2"],"Constant acceleration means a = (v − v0)/t. Solving this defining relation for final velocity gives v = v0 + at, with every term having velocity units.");
  p("U1","1.5","Vectors and Motion in Two Dimensions","2.A","A projectile has launch speed v at angle θ above horizontal. Which expression gives its initial horizontal velocity component?","vx = v cos(θ)",["vx = v sin(θ)","vx = v tan(θ)","vx = v/cos(θ)"],"The horizontal component is adjacent to the launch angle in the velocity-component triangle, so cosine gives vx/v and therefore vx = v cosθ.");
  p("U2","2.5","Newton's Second Law","2.A","An object of mass m experiences forces Fright and Fleft with Fright larger. Which expression gives its acceleration to the right?","a = (Fright - Fleft)/m",["a = m(Fright - Fleft)","a = (Fright + Fleft)/m","a = m/(Fright - Fleft)"],"Taking right as positive gives Fnet = Fright − Fleft. Newton's second law a = Fnet/m then yields a = (Fright − Fleft)/m.");
  p("U2","2.9","Circular Motion","2.A","A mass m travels at speed v around a circle of radius r. Which expression gives the required inward net force?","Fin = mv^2/r",["Fin = mvr","Fin = mr/v^2","Fin = mv/r^2"],"Centripetal acceleration is ac = v²/r. Substituting it into Fnet = ma produces the inward net-force expression Fin = mv²/r.");
  p("U3","3.2","Work","2.A","A constant force F acts at angle θ to a displacement d. Which expression gives the work done by that force?","W = Fd cos(θ)",["W = Fd sin(θ)","W = Fd/θ","W = F cos(θ)/d"],"Work is the dot product of force and displacement, so only the parallel force component F cosθ contributes. Multiplying by displacement gives W = Fd cosθ.");
  p("U3","3.4","Conservation of Energy","2.A","A block starts from rest and descends vertical height h without friction. Which expression gives its speed at the bottom?","v = sqrt(2gh)",["v = 2gh","v = sqrt(gh/2)","v = gh^2"],"Conservation gives mgh = (1/2)mv². Canceling mass and solving for the positive speed produces v = √(2gh).");
  p("U4","4.2","Change in Momentum and Impulse","2.A","A constant net force F acts for time interval Δt. Which expression gives the resulting change in momentum?","Δp = F Δt",["Δp = F/Δt","Δp = Δt/F","Δp = F(Δt)^2"],"The impulse-momentum theorem is Δp = ∫F dt. For constant force, the integral reduces to Δp = FΔt, including the force direction.");
  p("U4","4.3","Conservation of Linear Momentum","2.A","Mass m1 moving at v1 sticks to stationary mass m2. Which expression gives their final velocity?","vf = m1 v1/(m1 + m2)",["vf = (m1 + m2)v1/m1","vf = m2 v1/(m1 + m2)","vf = m1 v1/m2"],"Momentum conservation gives m1v1 = (m1 + m2)vf because the second mass starts at rest. Solving yields vf = m1v1/(m1 + m2).");
  p("U5","5.3","Torque","2.A","A force F acts a distance r from an axis at angle θ to the radius vector. Which expression gives torque magnitude?","τ = rF sin(θ)",["τ = rF cos(θ)","τ = F/(r sin(θ))","τ = rF/θ"],"Torque magnitude comes from the perpendicular force component F sinθ, or equivalently the lever arm r sinθ. Thus τ = rF sinθ.");
  p("U5","5.4","Rotational Inertia","2.A","Point masses mi are located distances ri from a fixed axis. Which expression gives the system's rotational inertia?","I = sum(mi ri^2)",["I = sum(mi ri)","I = sum(mi/ri^2)","I = sum(ri^2/mi)"],"Each point mass contributes mr² to rotational inertia because distance from the axis is weighted quadratically. Adding all contributions gives I = Σmiri².");
  p("U6","6.3","Angular Momentum and Angular Impulse","2.A","A rigid body with rotational inertia I rotates about its symmetry axis at angular speed ω. Which expression gives angular momentum magnitude?","L = I ω",["L = I/ω","L = ω/I","L = I ω^2"],"For a rigid system rotating about the specified symmetry axis, angular momentum magnitude is the product of rotational inertia and angular speed: L = Iω.");
  p("U6","6.4","Conservation of Angular Momentum","2.A","A system changes from rotational inertia Ii and angular speed ωi to inertia If with negligible external torque. Which expression gives ωf?","ωf = Ii ωi/If",["ωf = If ωi/Ii","ωf = Ii If/ωi","ωf = ωi/(Ii If)"],"Conservation requires Iiωi = Ifωf. Dividing both sides by If produces the final-speed expression ωf = Iiωi/If.");
  p("U7","7.2","Frequency and Period of SHM","2.A","A mass m oscillates on an ideal spring of constant k. Which expression gives its period?","T = 2 π sqrt(m/k)",["T = 2 π sqrt(k/m)","T = 2 π m/k","T = sqrt(mk)/(2 π)"],"The mass-spring angular frequency is ω = √(k/m), and period is 2π/ω. Substitution gives T = 2π√(m/k).");
  p("U7","7.4","Energy of Simple Harmonic Oscillators","2.A","An ideal spring oscillator has spring constant k and amplitude A. Which expression gives its total mechanical energy?","E = one-half k A^2",["E = kA","E = one-half k^2 A","E = k/(2A^2)"],"At a turning point speed is zero and displacement magnitude is A, so all energy is elastic: E = Us = (1/2)kA².");
  p("U8","8.3","Fluids and Newton's Laws","2.A","An object displaces volume V of a fluid with density ρ. Which expression gives buoyant-force magnitude?","Fb = ρ g V",["Fb = ρ V/g","Fb = gV/ρ","Fb = ρ g/V"],"Archimedes' principle equates buoyant force to the displaced fluid's weight. That fluid has mass ρV, so its weight and the buoyant force are ρgV.");
  p("U8","8.4","Fluids and Conservation Laws","2.A","Ideal incompressible flow passes from area A1 at speed v1 into area A2. Which expression gives v2?","v2 = A1 v1/A2",["v2 = A2 v1/A1","v2 = A1 A2/v1","v2 = v1/(A1 A2)"],"Conservation of mass for steady incompressible flow gives A1v1 = A2v2. Dividing by A2 yields v2 = A1v1/A2.");

